<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gordion</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Gordium</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gordion was the capital of ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phrygia">Phrygia</a>, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sakarya-river">Sakarya River</a> west of modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ankara">Ankara</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>. Its citadel, with large megaron halls, was destroyed by fire around 800 BCE and rebuilt. The great burial mound known as Tumulus MM, built around 740 BCE, held a king, possibly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/midas">Midas</a>' father, with bronze vessels and wooden furniture. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a> cut the Gordian Knot there in 333 BCE. It is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gordion.jpg" alt="Gordion" width="320" height="230" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gordion Archive, Penn Museum · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gordion_Early_Phrygian_East_Gate.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Gordion lies at the site of modern Yassıhüyük, 70 to 80 kilometres south-west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ankara">Ankara</a>, where the Sakarya and Porsuk rivers meet. The ancient road between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lydia">Lydia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonia">Babylonia</a> crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sakarya-river">Sangarius</a> river here. People lived on the site from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/early-bronze-age">Early Bronze Age</a>, about 2300 BCE, until the 4th century CE, and again in the 13th and 14th centuries. The Citadel Mound covers about 13.5 hectares, and at its largest the settlement spread over about 100 hectares.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In the Late Bronze Age Gordion belonged to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hittite-empire">Hittite Empire</a>, at the western edge of its heartland. A circuit wall and the East Citadel Gate were built in the Early Phrygian period of the 10th and 9th centuries BCE, and the gate still stands ten metres high, the best preserved in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>. Tumulus W, about 850 BCE, is the first known tumulus burial in Anatolia. The megaron halls on the citadel include some of the earliest known pebble-mosaic floors. A fire about 800 BCE sealed this level. It was once blamed on a Cimmerian attack around 700 BCE, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/radiocarbon-dating">radiocarbon dating</a> and tree-ring analysis show it was about a century earlier and probably accidental.</p><p>After the fire the citadel was rebuilt on a similar plan, and the city grew into the capital of a kingdom controlling much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a> west of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kizilirmak">Halys</a>. Midas was probably on the throne by about 740 BCE, and Assyrian sources of about 718 to 709 BCE call him Mit-ta-a. Tumulus MM, over fifty metres high and about three hundred metres across, was built about 740 BCE. Excavated in 1957, it held a royal burial on purple and golden textiles in a log coffin, with bronze vessels and well-preserved carved wooden furniture. It is generally assumed to be the tomb of Midas' father Gordias.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyrus-the-great">Cyrus the Great</a>'s forces besieged Gordion in 546 BCE, and it passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire</a>. Alexander the Great came in 333 BCE, and by tradition cut the Gordian Knot, which tied the yoke to the pole of a wagon on the acropolis; a prophecy said that whoever loosened it would rule Asia. Galatians settled there from the mid-3rd century BCE. The site was abandoned in the 1st century BCE and refounded under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Gustav and Alfred Körte excavated the site in 1900, and the University of Pennsylvania Museum worked there from 1950 under Rodney S. Young. Finds are displayed in the Museum of Anatolian Civilizations in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ankara">Ankara</a>, the Istanbul Archaeological Museums and the Gordion Museum in Yassıhöyük. Gordion was listed as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> in 2023.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Gordion” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>According to Strabo and others, Midas killed himself by drinking bulls' blood when the Cimmerians overran his kingdom and sacked <b>Gordium</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-phrygia">Kingdom of Phrygia · At a glance</a></li></ul></section>
